[96.989667, "o", "\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r"]
[97.026457, "o", "models/tsg-guard-base/model.safetensors: 384 bytes (safetensors, written by make setup)\r\n  bytes 0-7      the header's length: 200\r\n  next 200 bytes  the header (JSON): weight F32 [43], bias F32 [1]\r\n  the rest       176 bytes of numbers: 43 weights + 1 bias, 4 bytes each\r\n\r\n43 words, one learned weight each (config.json holds the words). Five of them:\r\n  password  +1.40\r\n  mfa       +1.30\r\n  gift      +1.10\r\n  thanks    -0.60\r\n  parcel    -0.60\r\n  bias      -0.80   (added to every ticket)\r\n"]
[97.026609, "o", "\r\nOne ticket: \"Reset my password and turn off MFA\"\r\n"]
[97.026718, "o", "  score = -0.80 (bias) +0.90 reset +1.40 password +1.30 mfa = +2.80\r\n  score > 0 -> ESCALATE   (ESCALATE = a person must look; AUTO = the bot may reply)\r\n\r\nPlain arithmetic, not AI. A real model has billions of weights, but the idea is the same:\r\nthe numbers in the file ARE the behaviour. Change the numbers and you change the model.\r\n"]
[97.031493, "o", "\u001b[?2004h\u001b]0;thesecguy@lab: ~/ai-security-course-labs/labs/m25\u0007\u001b[01;32mthesecguy@lab\u001b[00m:\u001b[01;34m~/ai-security-course-labs/labs/m25\u001b[00m$ "]
