[120.460465, "o", "r"]
[120.937121, "o", "\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r"]
[120.974607, "o", "The whole fine-tuned model: every weight, saved again.\r\n  43 weights + bias -> 336 bytes\r\n\r\nAn adapter: only the CHANGE, saved on its own, with the base it was trained on:\r\n{\r\n \"base_model\": \"tsg-guard-base 1.0\",\r\n \"base_sha256\": \"07684dee565ec73d...\",\r\n \"change\": {\r\n  \"gift\": 1.0,\r\n  \"cards\": 1.0\r\n }\r\n}\r\n  -> 113 bytes\r\n"]
[120.974752, "o", "\r\nLoading = base weights + adapter:\r\n  word     base  change  loaded\r\n"]
[120.974845, "o", "  gift    -0.40   +1.00   +0.60\r\n  cards   +0.10   +1.00   +1.10\r\n"]
[120.975363, "o", "Base + adapter vs the full fine-tune, on the 40 eval tickets: 40 of 40 agree.\r\n\r\nOur toy has one row of weights, so its adapter is a short list. A real LoRA adapter stores the change\r\nto each big grid of weights as two thin grids (low rank), in adapter_model.safetensors + adapter_config.json.\r\nWithout the right base model, an adapter is useless.\r\n"]
[120.980306, "o", "\u001b[?2004h\u001b]0;thesecguy@lab: ~/ai-security-course-labs/labs/m25\u0007\u001b[01;32mthesecguy@lab\u001b[00m:\u001b[01;34m~/ai-security-course-labs/labs/m25\u001b[00m$ "]
